    "proposal": {
      "plan": "Goal: Implement `add(a, b)` in `answer.py` so it returns `a + b` for positive, negative, and zero inputs.\n\nImplementation proposal:\n- Create a new file `answer.py` at the workspace root.\n- Define a function `def add(a, b):`.\n- Implement it as a pure function returning the sum of the two parameters: `return a + b`.\n- Keep the implementation minimal and deterministic (no side effects, no I/O).\n- If the repository\u2019s test harness expects a specific export name, ensure the function name is exactly `add`.\n\nVerification plan:\n- Add/execute unit-style checks (via the existing test runner or by adding a small local test if tests are absent).\n- Verify correctness with explicit input cases covering:\n  - Positive + positive: `add(2, 3) == 5`\n  - Negative + negative: `add(-2, -3) == -5`\n  - Mixed signs: `add(2, -3) == -1` and `add(-2, 3) == 1`\n  - Zero cases: `add(0, 5) == 5`, `add(5, 0) == 5`, `add(0, 0) == 0`\n- (Optional) Type handling assumptions:\n  - If the harness may pass integers only, ensure behavior matches integer addition.\n  - If floats could be passed, confirm `a + b` works for numeric types without extra casting.\n- Ensure the function returns a numeric value (no string conversion).\n\nOut of scope:\n- No changes to README or other files beyond adding `answer.py` (unless the test harness requires additional integration).",
